Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 06:33:52 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 16/16] media: cedrus: vc1: take the reference state from the
 picture layer control

The intensity compensation registers of a picture (PICINTENCOMP,
PICICBAK0, PICICBAK1, the INTENCOMPFLD field and, for frame pictures,
INTENSITY_COMP_EN) depend on the compensation which earlier pictures
applied to the reference fields. The driver kept that in five values
saved in the context from one decode to the next, as the vendor library
does. The stateless uAPI passes it with every request instead: the
forward_ref and backward_ref descriptions of the picture layer control
list, per reference field, the compensations that earlier pictures
applied to it, in the order in which they apply.

Rebuild the register values from the descriptions:

  PICICBAK1    what the second field of the forward reference frame did
               to its first field
  PICICBAK0    what the first field (or the frame) of the next P picture
               did to the forward reference: the first field of the
               current frame for a P field, the backward reference for
               a B field
  PICINTENCOMP of a P picture: its own compensation; of a B field: what
               the second field of the backward reference did to the
               forward reference and to its own first field
  B frames     the compensation of the backward reference (the vendor
               keeps the register of the last P frame)

The register layout is the one of "fix field intensity compensation
slots": one slot per field parity, the default scale for a field which
is not compensated, and the field mask in the backup registers.

The descriptions do not say which picture signalled each listed
compensation. That follows from which pictures could have compensated
the field (field coding, picture types and field order of the
references, FPTYPE of the current frame); when fewer are listed than
could have been, the most recent pictures are taken to be the ones
which did. Only the fields of the forward reference which the picture
reads are programmed: a second field reads the first field of its own
frame instead of the forward reference field of the other parity.

Differences from the saved state, all in field-coded content with
intensity compensation:
 - an I field no longer forgets the compensation pending on the
   previous reference (B fields after an I/P anchor keep it);
 - the backup registers only hold the fields which the current picture
   reads, not the whole register of the picture which signalled them;
 - skipped pictures, which are not submitted, carry no compensation.

This is not tested on hardware. Comparing the decoder output with and
without this patch on field-coded streams with intensity compensation
tells whether the hardware agrees with the model.

